            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Is the easier answer to just set the to limit to 1000?  2000?\n\nI'm not very comfortable just treating the filesystem as trusted with no limits.\n\nThe 500 limit in sax parse is arbitrary, and was only really done in response to some security issues reported.  500 was intended to be 2X the largest value expected, nowhere near the real limit, but you're right, I didn't think about session storage for a large number of sessions (although we're still pretty close).\n\nLong term I'd really like to just split each session ID into a separate persistent json file so we're not reading/writing every session anytime one is added, and any corruption can't take out the whole persistent file.  If we did that it would also make this moot, but that would be a larger effort.\n\nLet me know if changing the limit to something larger tracks for you."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "As an aside, this also prevents me from updating our astgrep rule and errors to outright ban nlohmann::json::parse, which I intended to do.  if we still have some invocations that are considered trusted, that's a more subtle rule to try to enforce."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "*ban only in bmcweb*"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Rick Yazwinski",
                        "email": "rickyaz@meta.com",
                        "username": "ryazwinski"
                    },
                    "message": "I really like the idea of individual files over just bumping the limit to the next arbitrary value and hoping. If we land this as is, I'll fast follow with the individual json file work."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "If we do separate files, this patchset is obsolete and we'd effectively revert it and go back to the sax parser. If we want to land a patch now to fix this, lets land just doubling the threshold (which is a one line change).\n\nFWIW, at NV downstream we've already had to 10X the limit because of very...very large bios registries that I didn't believe effected upstream.  It's a relatively easy change to make.  If you need help I'm happy to do it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Rick Yazwinski",
                        "email": "rickyaz@meta.com",
                        "username": "ryazwinski"
                    },
                    "message": "ok, sounds good.  I'll abandon this and bump the limit"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 17,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Thank you."
                }
            ],
